onths-old Democratic-sponsored hypothetical"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, a favorable national environment for the GOP, and a ratings consensus of Lean R point toward the Republican, and prediction markets agree at roughly a one-in-three chance for Democrats. Dalton's clear money advantage and Elijah's fresh, untested nomination after ousting Mills keep this genuinely competitive, so the forecast sits only modestly on the Republican side of even.", "watch": "Any credible general-election polling, or signs that Dalton's fundraising edge is converting into paid media while Elijah remains underfunded, could shift this toward a Democratic lean.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Cory Mills (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 7th District is an open seat this fall after Republican Cory Mills lost his party's nomination to Ryan Elijah, who now faces Democrat Bale Dalton. The district carries a Republican lean of about five points, and the political environment this year has generally favored Republicans, giving the GOP nominee a structural edge. Expert raters mostly call the race Lean or Likely Republican, though a couple rate it a Tossup and one has flagged a Democratic flip.\n\nDalton has out-raised Elijah by roughly four to one and benefits from outside spending on his side, while Elijah enters with a thinner war chest and a nomination fight behind him. Prediction markets still price the Republican as the clear favorite. With no reliable public polling, the race is best described as a modest Republican edge with real uncertainty, closer to a Tossup than a safe seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4211, "margin": -1.21}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4651, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3497, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3248, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0